{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2462 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "12fcd1253acd9b7f4c49675424d749dc68d7639b7ffc1d19d5f58440238b1014" :edb/db-before-t 2461 :edb/db-after-t 2462 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535774 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:00:03.593Z" 13194139535774 true] [17592186151892 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 4y = 0,\\quad x > 0.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186151892 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 4y = 0,\\quad x > 0$." 13194139535774 false] [17592186151892 1145 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + x \\cdot λx^{λ - 1} + 4x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nλ(λ - 1)x^{λ} + λx^{λ} + 4x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{λ}[λ(λ - 1) + λ + 4] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ(λ - 1) + λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ &= ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=c_1 \\cos(2 \\ln{x}) + c_2\\sin(2 \\ln{x})\n$$" 13194139535774 false] [17592186151892 1145 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=x^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +4] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=c_1 \\cos( 2 \\ln{x} )  + c_2\\sin( 2 \\ln{x} ).\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186151893 1100 "Determine the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 y'' +5ty'+8y = 0,\\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186151893 1100 "Determine the general solution to the equation\n\n$t^2 y'' +5ty'+8y = 0,\\quad t\\gt 0$." 13194139535774 false] [17592186151893 1145 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=t^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)t^{λ - 2} + 5t \\cdot λt^{λ - 1} + 8t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nλ(λ - 1)t^{λ} + 5λt^{λ} + 8t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nt^{λ}[λ(λ - 1) + 5λ + 8] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ(λ - 1) + 5λ + 8 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 4λ + 8 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 4λ + 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 4λ + 4) - 4 + 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ + 2 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -2 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t) = t^{-2} \\left(c_1 \\cos(2 \\ln{t}) + c_2 \\sin(2 \\ln{t}) \\right)\n$$" 13194139535774 false] [17592186151893 1145 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=t^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+5t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +8] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t) = t^{-2} \\left( c_1 \\cos( 2 \\ln{t} ) + c_2 \\sin( 2 \\ln{t} ) \\right).\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186151894 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)= c_1 \\cos(7 \\ln{x} )  + c_2\\sin(7 \\ln{x} ),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1) = -7, \\quad x > 0.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186151894 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y(x)= c_1 \\cos(7 \\ln{x}) + c_2\\sin(7 \\ln{x})$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1) = -7, \\quad x > 0$." 13194139535774 false] [17592186151894 1145 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0 = c_1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -7,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{7}{x}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -7$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =7{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = -\\sin(7 \\ln{x} ).\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186151894 1145 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -7$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y^{′}|= \\frac{7}{x}(c_{2}\\cos (7\\ln x) - c_{1}\\sin (7\\ln x))$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -7$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above to get\n$-7|= 7c_{2}; c_{2}|=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = -\\sin(7 \\ln{x})\n$$" 13194139535774 false] [17592186151895 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = t(c_1 \\cos( 3 \\ln{t} ) + c_2\\sin( 3 \\ln{t}) ),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0,  \\quad y'(1) = -1, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186151895 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = t(c_1 \\cos(3 \\ln{t}) + c_2\\sin(3 \\ln{t}))$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0, \\quad y'(1) = -1, \\quad t\\gt 0$." 13194139535774 false] [17592186151895 1145 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0  = c_1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -1$ and $c_1=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3{c}_{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & ={c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t) = -\\dfrac{ t\\sin( 3 \\ln{t}) )}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186151895 1145 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -1$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y^{′}|= c_{2}(\\sin (3\\ln t) + 3\\cos (3\\ln t))$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -1$ and $c_1=0$ into the above to get\n$-1|= 3c_{2}; - \\frac{1}{3}|= c_{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t) = -\\dfrac{t\\sin(3 \\ln{t}))}{3}\n$$" 13194139535774 false] [17592186152095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365470 13194139535774 true] [17592186152095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365512 13194139535774 true] [17592186152095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365519 13194139535774 true] [17592186152095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365526 13194139535774 true] [17592186152096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365477 13194139535774 true] [17592186152096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365484 13194139535774 true] [17592186152096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365533 13194139535774 true] [17592186152096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365540 13194139535774 true] [17592186152097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365491 13194139535774 true] [17592186152097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365547 13194139535774 true] [17592186152097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365554 13194139535774 true] [17592186152097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365561 13194139535774 true] [17592186152098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365498 13194139535774 true] [17592186152098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365505 13194139535774 true] [17592186152098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365568 13194139535774 true] [17592186152098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365575 13194139535774 true] [17592186152531 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535774 true] [17592186152531 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186152531 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} + y = 0, x > 0$." 13194139535774 false] [17592186152531 1145 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{\\text{i}}}={e}^{\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x), \\\\ {x}^{-\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{-\\text{i}}}={e}^{-\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-\\text{i}} \\\\ y(x) & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ y(x) & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(x)$ as\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\n$$\n\nwhere  ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186152534 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535774 true] [17592186152534 1100 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-2,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186152534 1100 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} + 5xy^{′} + 5y = 0, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) =-2, x > 0$." 13194139535774 false] [17592186152534 1145 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+5x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+5{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+5{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +5] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =-1 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2\\pm \\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-2+\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{-2-\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{-2+\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-2-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}+B\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{{c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-2.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=1$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{({c}_{2}-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-(1+2{c}_{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & ={c}_{2}-2 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186152536 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535774 true] [17592186152536 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+6y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186152536 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$2t^{2}y^{″} - 2ty^{′} + 6y = 0, t > 0$." 13194139535774 false] [17592186152536 1145 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-2t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-2\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2\\lambda +6] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ 2({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1)-1+3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{1+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}} & =t{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)), \\\\ {t}^{1-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}} & =t{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{1+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}+B{t}^{1-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =At(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+Bt(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B)t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B)t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(t)$ as \n\n$$\ny(t)=t({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186152537 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535774 true] [17592186152537 1100 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+32y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139535774 true] [17592186152537 1100 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$2x^{2}y^{″} + 2xy^{′} + 32y = 0, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) = 0, x > 0$." 13194139535774 false] [17592186152537 1145 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+2x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+32{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+2\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+32{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+2\\lambda +32] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+2\\lambda +32 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}+32 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}.$  \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{x}^{4\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-4\\text{i}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{4\\text{i}}}={e}^{4\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \